  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Proposed Guarantees, Loans, and Risk Participation Products for Additional Financing Trade Finance Program</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ADB&apos;s Trade Finance Program (TFP) was established by ADB in 2003 with a $150 million limit. In 2009, following the global financial crisis, the ADB Board of Directors endorsed an expansion of the program limit to $1 billion. In 2012, the Board endorsed a major change in the program removing the sunset clause and introducing a maximum 3-year reporting requirement. In January 2016, ADB circulated an information paper to the Board incorporating a review of the program for 2012-2015 and a projection for 2016-2018. In 2018, the Board endorsed an expansion of the program limit to $1.35 billion. In April 2020, under ADB&apos;s Comprehensive Response to the COVID-19 Pandemic, the ADB Board of Directors approved some special policy variations and the program limit increase up to $2.15 billion. The objective was to provide TFP with the enabling framework and the financial headroom to mount a robust response to the pandemic. In August 2020, ADB&apos;&apos;s Trade and Supply Chain Finance Programs announced the merger of their operations. The two programs&apos; joint effort to reduce financial gaps was renamed as the Trade and Supply Chain Finance Program (TSCFP), acknowledging ADB&apos;s commitment to further support DMCs banks by enhancing the product offering. Notwithstanding the above, TFP and SCFP operate under separate Board approvals.</narrative>
  </description>
